<h2>Problem Solving With Perimeter and Area</h2>
<p><a href="https://cms-assets.illustrativemathematics.org/ytq1htcawoielc5879qjec406n6f" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Original 2021 student task PDF (3 pages)</a>. First compare division expressions: true, true, false, false, with teacher explanations.</p>
<p>Garden: 30-ft by 8-ft rectangle. To find length of rope, add each boundary edge or double the sum of one length and one width. Multiplying 30×8 gives AREA, not rope length. Discuss why Clare and Diego could <strong>both</strong> have valid methods if multiplication represents grouped side lengths.</p>
<h3>Teacher-led Information Gap</h3>
<p>The original Activity 10.2 includes TWO distinct problem/data card pairs. Your teacher gives each partner exactly one card, separately. The problem student asks what information is needed and explains why; the data-card student responds but does not show the card. Pause for teacher review, then switch roles with the second set. No original card answers are displayed on this page.</p>
<p><strong>Measurement:</strong> perimeter uses metres (m) or feet (ft), while area uses square metres (m²) or square feet (ft²). Both can involve side length calculations but answer different questions.</p>
<p>Original cards, teacher dialogue, access and correction remain teacher-led. The original gated publisher Cool-down is NOT reproduced.</p>
